AUX-CONTEXT|<work>/refute-out/run/aux_src_single_sided_lp_AbstractSingleSidedLP_sol_finalizeAndRedeemWithdrawRequest.txt
Trace of the actual flow:

  1. The only way an account reaches the escrowed/pending state is __initiateWithdraw → ILPLib.initiateWithdraw. That
  loop is: if (exitBalances[i] == 0) continue; manager = registry.get(tokens[i]);
  tokens[i].checkApprove(address(manager), ...); manager.initiateWithdraw({account, yieldTokenAmount: exitBalances[i],
  sharesAmount: sharesHeld, ...}).
  2. Therefore, in the "only the illiquid leg has a registered manager" configuration the claim leans on, the state it
  needs can never be created: __initiateWithdraw exits the pool with isSingleSided: false (proportional), so the
  manager-less leg has a nonzero exitBalances[i], the continue does not fire, and the call hits checkApprove(address(0),
  amt) / IWithdrawRequestManager(address(0)).initiateWithdraw(...) and reverts before any escrow exists. No pending
  request, no escrowed shares, no LP value handed to any manager — nothing to freeze. The escrowed branch of
  _redeemShares is simply never entered for that config.
  3. In every configuration where initiateWithdraw does succeed, each leg it touched got a request created with
  sharesAmount: sharesHeld (nonzero, since the account holds shares) and a nonzero manager. So on the subsequent
  finalizeAndRedeemWithdrawRequest pass, both manager != address(0) and w.sharesAmount != 0 hold for exactly the legs
  that exist — the division w.yieldTokenAmount * sharesToRedeem / w.sharesAmount has a nonzero denominator and the
  external call has code behind it. The absent guards are absent precisely because the state they would guard against
  cannot coexist with a successful initiate.
  4. The residual "one-legged request" variant requires a leg whose proportional exit balance rounds to exactly zero
  while the other leg is nonzero, in a two-token Curve pool where both legs have registered managers. The claim names no
  unprivileged mechanism producing that; a proportional withdrawal from a two-token pool yields dust on both sides
  unless one reserve is driven to sub-wei relative to the vault's share, which is not an attacker-controllable step
  shown anywhere in the traced code. Whether a manager is registered for a given token is registry configuration set by
  a privileged role, not by the attacker.
  5. The sibling comparison the claim makes is also not evidence of a bug: hasPendingWithdrawals and
  tokenizeWithdrawRequest are called speculatively on arbitrary accounts/configs (including accounts that never
  withdrew), so they must tolerate missing managers; getWithdrawRequestValue — the valuation counterpart of the finalize
  path — likewise has no zero-manager guard and instead does require(hasRequest) for every token, confirming that the
  all-legs-have-requests invariant is the deliberate, protocol-wide assumption on the pending-withdraw path rather than
  an oversight unique to this function.

  Under the tie-break, the precondition state is not shown to be reachable by an unprivileged caller, and the
  state-creating path provably reverts first in the configuration the claim cites.

  CONSTRAINT|class=C15|A claim that a missing guard permanently freezes funds must demonstrate that the precondition
  state is reachable by an unprivileged actor, and must survive the fact that the same unguarded assumption on the
  state-creating path causes that path to revert before the state can ever be established.
  VERDICT|REFUTED|src/single-sided-lp/AbstractSingleSidedLP.sol:finalizeAndRedeemWithdrawRequest|class=C15|The escrowed
  state can only be created by initiateWithdraw, which itself calls manager.initiateWithdraw (after checkApprove) on
  every leg with a nonzero proportional exit balance and so reverts outright when a leg's manager is unregistered —
  meaning any account that actually holds a pending request has, on every leg the finalize loop visits, both a nonzero
  manager and a nonzero w.sharesAmount (set to sharesHeld), so neither the division-by-zero nor the zero-address call is
  reachable.
